.TH FAAM 1 "2026-10-07" "1.0.0" "Freeware Advanced Audio Muxer"

.SH NAME
faam \- MP4 muxer, demuxer and tag editor for AAC audio and H.264/H.265 video

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B faam
.B \-i
.I input
.RB [ \-i
.IR input ...]
.B \-o
.I out.mp4
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B faam
.RB { info | dump }
.I file
.br
.B faam demux
.I file
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B faam tag
.I file
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B faam chapter
.RB { import | export }
.I file
.RI [ options ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B FAAM
reads and writes ISO BMFF files: MP4, M4A and M4B.
It is a front end to the
.B libfaam
library, licensed under the LGPL license.
It does not encode or decode audio or video.
The subcommands are:
.TP
.B \-i, \-o
With no subcommand word, mux one or more elementary streams (ADTS AAC,
Annex-B H.264 or H.265) into an MP4.
.TP
.B info
Print container, track, tag, gapless and chapter summaries.
.TP
.B dump
Print the MP4 atom tree.
.TP
.B demux
Extract one track's elementary stream.
.TP
.B tag
Edit iTunes metadata tags in place.
.TP
.B chapter
Import or export the chapters of an audiobook.
.PP
Each subcommand prints a result line to stdout on success and an error
message to stderr on failure.
H.264 and H.265 need a build with the
.B muxer-video
option (enabled by default); without it muxing video fails with an error.
.B faam
writes progressive files only.
Fragmented MP4 files can be read by
.BR info ,
.B dump
and
.B demux
in a build with the
.B muxer-fragmented
option (enabled by default).

.SH HELP OPTIONS
\-\-help\-<group> and \-\-license must be the first argument.
.TP
.BR \-h ,\  \-\-help
Display a short help text.
After a subcommand, display the options of that subcommand.
.TP
.BR \-H
Display a description of all options.
.TP
.BR \-\-help\-commands ,\  \-\-help\-mux ,\  \-\-help\-inspect ,\  \-\-help\-tag ,\  \-\-help\-chapter
Display the description of one group of options.
.TP
.BR \-\-license
Display copyright and license information.

.SH MUX OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-i ,\  \-\-input\ <\fIfile\fP>
Elementary stream to mux: ADTS AAC, or Annex-B H.264 or H.265.
Repeat for more tracks, up to 8.
.TP
.BR \-o ,\  \-\-output\ <\fIfile\fP>
Output MP4 file.
Required.
An output that exists is refused unless
.B \-\-overwrite
is given.
An output that is one of the inputs, spelled another way or reached through a
link, is refused even with
.BR \-\-overwrite .
A failed mux removes the partial output.
.TP
.BR \-\-overwrite
Overwrite an existing output file.
.TP
.BR \-\-codec:\fIN\fP\ <\fIcodec\fP>
Codec of the Nth
.BR \-i ,
counting from 0: aac, h264 (or avc) or h265 (or hevc).
Without it the codec follows the file extension:
.BR .264 / .h264
for H.264,
.BR .265 / .hevc
for H.265, anything else AAC.
.TP
.BR \-\-brand\ <\fIname\fP>
.B m4b
writes an audiobook brand;
.B m4a
is the default.
Anything else is an error.
Files with a video track use the
.B isom
brand regardless.
.TP
.BR \-\-width\ <\fIpixels\fP> ,\  \-\-height\ <\fIpixels\fP>
Size of video tracks, 1 to 65535.
Required for video, as the stream is not parsed for it.
.TP
.BR \-\-rotation\ <\fIdegrees\fP>
Display rotation of video tracks, clockwise: 0 (the default), 90, 180 or 270.
It is written to the track's display matrix; the pictures are not rotated.
.TP
.BR \-\-fps\ <\fIn[/d]\fP>
Frame rate of video tracks, a whole number or a fraction such as 30000/1001.
The default is 30.
.TP
.BR \-\-encoder\-delay\ <\fIsamples\fP>
Leading priming samples of the audio, written as gapless information
(an
.B iTunSMPB
atom and an edit list).
An error without an audio track.
The value is in samples at the track's sample rate, which for HE-AAC is the
core rate, half the output rate.
The encoder reports it: see
.BR EXAMPLES .
.TP
.BR \-\-padding\-delay\ <\fIsamples\fP>
Trailing padding samples of the audio, written as gapless information.
The length of the programme is derived from the frames muxed minus the delay
and the padding, so without this option the padding is not trimmed.
.TP
.BR \-\-sbr\-signaling\ <\fImode\fP>
ADTS cannot say whether SBR or PS follow, so the caller does.
.B none
(the default) leaves it to the decoder,
.B compatible
writes backward-compatible signaling,
.B explicit
hierarchical signaling,
.B ps
and
.B ps-explicit
the same for HE-AAC v2, which needs a mono core.
Anything else is an error.
.TP
.BR \-\-language\ <\fIcode3\fP> ,\  \-\-lang\ <\fIcode3\fP>
ISO 639-2/T language code of the audio tracks, for example eng or ger.
It must be three letters, in either case; the default is und.
.TP
.BR \-\-creation\-time\ <\fIvalue\fP>
Creation and modification time of the file.
.B auto
uses the modification time of the first input,
.B now
the current time, and a number is a Unix time in seconds that fits 32 bits;
larger or negative values are refused.
Without the option the
.B SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
environment variable is used when set, otherwise the time is left unset (0).
An invalid value gives a warning and 0.
.PP
Every option listed under
.B TAG OPTIONS
except
.B \-\-remove
and
.B \-\-clear
also applies when muxing.
.PP
ADTS input:
.IP \(bu 3
The AAC object type, sample rate and channel configuration are read from the
first ADTS header, which must be valid and must follow an optional ID3v2 tag.
.IP \(bu 3
Each frame is muxed as one 1024-sample unit, so a stream whose frames hold more
than one raw data block (number_of_raw_data_blocks above 0) is an error, as is
a channel configuration of 0.
.IP \(bu 3
Bytes that are not a frame header are skipped, and so are frames whose
profile, rate or channels differ from the first header's, as they are most
likely false syncs.
.IP \(bu 3
The
.B ps
modes need a mono core.
.PP
Video input:
.IP \(bu 3
One picture is muxed per sample in stream order.
The avcC or hvcC is built from the parameter sets in the stream, which must
hold them.
.IP \(bu 3
B-frames are not supported: no composition time offsets are written, so
reordered pictures would play out of order.
.IP \(bu 3
\-\-width and \-\-height are required.

.SH INSPECT AND EXTRACT OPTIONS
.B info
and
.B dump
take only the file name.
.B info
prints, in this order and omitting sections the file does not have:
.PP
.nf
Container: ISO BMFF (major brand M4A)
Tracks Count: 1

Track #1 (ID 1):
  Type: Audio
  Codec: AAC
  Timescale: 44100
  Sample Rate: 44100 Hz
  Channels: 2
  AAC Object Type: 2 (AAC-LC)
  SBR Present: No
  PS Present: No
  Total Frames: 131

Metadata Tags:
  Title: Song
  Track: 3/12

Gapless Audio Metadata:
  Encoder Delay: 1024 samples
  Trailing Padding: 820 samples

Chapters / Bookmarks (2 entries):
  Chapter #1: start=0 ms, title="Intro"
  Chapter #2: start=90500 ms, title="Part 2"
.fi
.PP
A track whose codec is not AAC, H.264 or H.265 is listed as Generic.
.B dump
lists the top-level boxes and what is inside
.BR moov .
.PP
.B demux
accepts:
.TP
.BR \-o ,\  \-\-output\ <\fIfile\fP>
Output file, output.raw by default.
A name containing
.B .aac
or
.B .adts
gets ADTS headers, built from the track's AudioSpecificConfig, on AAC frames;
video is written as Annex-B.
An output that exists is refused unless
.B \-\-overwrite
is given; an output that is the input is always refused.
A failed run removes the partial output.
.TP
.BR \-t ,\  \-\-track\ <\fIid\fP>
Track ID to extract, the first track by default.
.TP
.BR \-\-export\-asc\ <\fIfile\fP>
Also write the track's codec configuration (AudioSpecificConfig, avcC or hvcC).
.TP
.BR \-\-overwrite
Overwrite an existing output or
.B \-\-export\-asc
file.

.SH TAG OPTIONS
.B faam tag
rewrites the tags of
.I file
in place.
Existing tags are kept unless removed, so only the given fields change;
tags libfaam does not model (such as grouping or description) and extra cover art images are preserved byte-for-byte in original relative order.
Files using QuickTime keys metadata (mdta handler) are refused without modification.
A file that cannot be read as an MP4 is refused and left untouched.
The edit is not crash-safe: work on a copy of a file that matters.
Text is converted to UTF-8.
All options except
.B \-\-remove
and
.B \-\-clear
also work when muxing.
.TP
.BR \-\-title ,\  \-\-artist ,\  \-\-album ,\  \-\-albumartist ,\  \-\-composer\ <\fItext\fP>
Set the field.
.TP
.BR \-\-titlesort ,\  \-\-artistsort ,\  \-\-albumsort ,\  \-\-albumartistsort ,\  \-\-composersort\ <\fItext\fP>
Set the sort name of the field.
.TP
.BR \-\-year ,\  \-\-comment\ <\fItext\fP>
Set the field.
.TP
.BR \-\-genre\ <\fIvalue\fP>
An ID3v1 genre number (0 to 255) or name sets both the numeric and the text
genre.
Anything else is stored as free text without the numeric atom.
.TP
.BR \-\-compilation
Mark the file as part of a compilation.
.TP
.BR \-\-track\ <\fIn[/total]\fP> ,\  \-\-disc\ <\fIn[/total]\fP>
Set the track or disc number.
.TP
.BR \-\-cover\-art\ <\fIfile\fP>
Cover image, GIF, JPEG or PNG, up to 32 MiB.
.TP
.BR \-\-tag\ <\fIname=value\fP>
Custom tag; the name and the value are separated by the first
.B =
or
.BR , .
Both must be non-empty.
Repeat for more.
.TP
.BR \-\-remove\ <\fIfield\fP>
Remove a field before the other options apply.
The fields are title, titlesort, artist, artistsort, album, albumsort,
albumartist, albumartistsort, composer, composersort, year, comment, genre,
compilation, track, disc, cover-art and custom (all custom tags).
Repeatable, up to 32 times.
An unknown field is an error.
.TP
.BR \-\-clear
Remove every existing tag and preserved atom before the other options apply.

.SH CHAPTER OPTIONS
.TP
.BR import\ <\fIfile\fP>
Replace the chapters of
.I file
with those of the
.B \-\-chapters
file.
The tags are kept.
.TP
.BR export\ <\fIfile\fP>
Write the chapters of
.I file
to the
.B \-o
file.
A file without chapters is an error.
.TP
.BR \-\-chapters\ <\fIfile\fP>
Chapter list to import.
.TP
.BR \-o ,\  \-\-output\ <\fIfile\fP>
Chapter list to write.
An output that exists is refused unless
.B \-\-overwrite
is given; the input file is always refused.
.TP
.BR \-\-overwrite
Overwrite an existing chapter list on export.
.PP
A chapter list has one line per chapter, a time, a tab and a title, as in
.BR "00:01:30.500<TAB>Chapter 2" .
The time is HH:MM:SS.mmm with one to three fractional digits, so
.B .5
is 500 ms.
At most 255 chapters are read, a list without chapters is an error, and titles
are cut at 255 bytes.
Chapters are stored as a Nero
.B chpl
atom.
Apple players do not read these chapters, and QuickTime chapter tracks are not
written.

.SH EXAMPLES
Gapless playback needs the encoder's priming and padding, which the encoder
reports; ADTS does not carry them.
The .m4a that
.BR faac (1)
writes records them, and
.B faam info
shows them as Encoder Delay and Trailing Padding.
For AAC-LC from faac the delay is 1024 samples.
For HE-AAC v1 it is 1040: faac reports 2080 at the output rate, and the file
counts samples at the core rate, which is half.
The padding depends on the length of the input.
.PP
Mux an AAC-LC ADTS stream from faac into an M4A with gapless information:
.IP
.B faam -i song.aac -o song.m4a --encoder-delay 1024 --padding-delay 820
.PP
Mux an HE-AAC v1 ADTS stream from faac, announcing SBR:
.IP
.B faam -i song.aac -o song.m4a --sbr-signaling compatible --encoder-delay 1040 --padding-delay 1418
.PP
Mux an audiobook:
.IP
.B faam -i book.aac -o book.m4b --brand m4b --title "Book" --language eng
.PP
Mux H.264 video and AAC audio:
.IP
.B faam -i clip.264 -i sound.aac -o clip.mp4 --width 1280 --height 720 --fps 30000/1001
.PP
Show what a file contains:
.IP
.B faam info song.m4a
.PP
Extract the audio as an ADTS stream:
.IP
.B faam demux song.m4a -o song.aac
.PP
Set tags and cover art:
.IP
.B faam tag song.m4a --title "Song" --artist "Artist" --track 3/12 --cover-art cover.jpg
.PP
Replace all tags:
.IP
.B faam tag song.m4a --clear --title "Song"
.PP
Copy chapters from one audiobook to another:
.IP
.B faam chapter export a.m4b -o chapters.txt
.br
.B faam chapter import b.m4b --chapters chapters.txt

.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success and
.B 1
on a usage or option error, an invalid option value, an unreadable input, an
uncreatable output, an output that exists without
.BR \-\-overwrite ,
an output that is also an input, an invalid chapter list, a file that
.B tag
cannot read, or a library error.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
Creation time of muxed files when
.B \-\-creation\-time
is not given.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR faac (1)
.PP
.BR faac (1)
is the AAC encoder.
It is built and installed unless the
.B encoder
build option is disabled.
